# Pulse Surveys — configuration surface (SPEC §6.3).
#
# This file is the configuration documentation. Copy it to `.env` for local
# development; `.env` is gitignored and never committed. Every value below is a
# placeholder — no real credential belongs in this file, ever (CLAUDE.md).
#
# This file has four readers, not one: `app.config.Settings`, the Compose files,
# `backend/migrations/env.py`, which reads the three database variables it needs
# so that `alembic` works from a stock checkout (ADR 0012), and `scripts/seed.py`,
# which reads those three and ENVIRONMENT so that `make seed` does too (ADR 0063,
# ADR 0008 as amended). The process environment beats this file for all four. A
# unit test holds the file in sync with the first two, in both directions: a
# setting with no entry here fails, and an entry here that neither a setting nor a
# Compose file reads fails — the other two deliberately introduce no name of their
# own, so they need no entry and vouch for none.
#
# The second half is checked by finding the reader, never by naming it: an entry
# passes because a `Settings` field resolves to it or because a Compose file
# interpolates it as `${NAME}`. Stop interpolating a name and the entry starts
# failing on the next run, which is how an abandoned line gets noticed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment wiring — required. No default exists for any of these, because a
# working default would be a misconfiguration that starts successfully.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The database roles, and the parts the URL below is built from. They are
# separate entries for one reason: `docker-compose.yml` has to hand Postgres its
# own credentials, and Compose cannot parse a URL. Splitting the inputs keeps
# each password written once; the alternative was a second copy that can drift
# out of step. See docs/adr/0008.
#
# No `Settings` field reads any of these. The application reads DATABASE_URL and
# CARE_DATABASE_URL and nothing else — `app.config.Settings` ignores extra
# variables precisely so that `.env` can be one file for the application and for
# Compose both.
#
# **These seven must stay above the two URLs below**, for a narrower reason than
# "the file is read top-down". The `${...}` references that configure the `db`
# service live in docker-compose.yml, and Compose resolves those against the
# whole environment, so their position here is irrelevant. What is
# order-sensitive is the nested expansion inside those URLs' own values below.
#
# So moving this block down does not break the stack, it splits it: the database
# is still created with the right user and password, while the application is
# handed `postgresql+psycopg://:@db:5432/`. Nothing complains at
# `docker compose up`, and it surfaces as an authentication error two tickets
# later. Measured on the running daemon, and a test asserts it.
#
# Three roles, not one, and each has a job (docs/adr/0009, docs/adr/0001).
#
# DB_SUPERUSER is the role `initdb` creates. It is the cluster superuser, and
# that is sanctioned: migrations and system-level tasks need it, and nothing
# else does. It is deliberately not delivered to the application container —
# `docker-compose.yml` blanks it on the `api` service.
#
# DB_APP_USER is what the application connects as, every day, and it must never
# be a superuser: one bypasses every grant and every row-level security policy,
# and can run `COPY … FROM PROGRAM`, which is command execution inside the
# database container. `scripts/db-init` creates it on first start and grants it
# CONNECT (SPEC §4.1, docs/adr/0001 line 71, which ADR 0009 leaves standing).
#
# DB_CARE_USER exists so that reaching a student's name is a different
# *connection* rather than a different query. SPEC §6.2 gives the Care queue to
# the Care role and only the Care role; ADR 0001 makes that a database grant, so
# the application holds two credentials and only one of them can execute the
# audited reveal. It is not a superuser either, and it holds no SELECT on the
# identity table — its whole reach is one function that writes an audit row in
# the same transaction as the read. `scripts/db-init` creates it on first start
# and the E0-10 migration sets its attributes and its grants, exactly as for
# DB_APP_USER. Unlike DB_APP_USER, it is blanked on every application container:
# only `db` reads these two, and CARE_DATABASE_URL below says why.
#
# **Neither URL below may point at DB_SUPERUSER, and they must not point at each
# other's role either.** DATABASE_URL is DB_APP_USER; CARE_DATABASE_URL is
# DB_CARE_USER. Pointing both at one role is the separation undone in one line.
DB_SUPERUSER=pulse_admin
DB_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=replace-me-admin
DB_APP_USER=pulse_app
DB_APP_PASSWORD=replace-me-app
DB_CARE_USER=pulse_care
DB_CARE_PASSWORD=replace-me-care
DB_NAME=pulse

# Application database. `postgresql+psycopg://user:password@host:port/database`.
#
# The host is the Compose service name, because the file this is copied to has
# to be one the stack can actually start from — CI does `cp .env.example .env`
# and then `docker compose up`. Running uvicorn on the host instead of in a
# container therefore needs this and REDIS_URL pointed back at localhost; the
# README says so where it tells you to do that.
#
# Both Compose and python-dotenv expand `${...}` here, so the parts above reach
# the container and the host process alike.
#
# The `+psycopg` driver is named here and in the migration-drift job, and is
# pinned in pyproject.toml as `psycopg[binary]` — the binary extra because the
# runtime image carries no libpq for the pure-Python distribution to find.
#
# Alembic reads this entry too, for the host, port and database it names, and
# then connects as DB_SUPERUSER rather than as the role in the URL (ADR 0009,
# ADR 0012). Running a migration from your own machine therefore needs a host
# this machine can resolve; README.md says where to point it.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://${DB_APP_USER}:${DB_APP_PASSWORD}@db:5432/${DB_NAME}

# The Care queue's connection to the same database, as a different role (§6.2,
# ADR 0001, ADR 0042). Same host, same database, same driver — the only thing
# that differs is who is connecting, and that is the whole point: `pulse_app`
# holds no privilege on the identity table and cannot execute the reveal, so an
# instructor screen physically cannot produce a name however it is written.
#
# Read by `app.services.safety` and by nothing else, and **only the API process
# is given it**. `docker-compose.yml` blanks this entry on `worker` and `beat`,
# which never serve this queue — `worker` is the process that ships comment text
# to a third-party model provider, so it is the last container that should hold a
# route to a student's name. The DB_CARE_USER and DB_CARE_PASSWORD parts above it
# are blanked on **every** application container including `api`, as the note at
# DB_CARE_USER says: only `db` needs the parts, and leaving them anywhere else
# would rebuild this URL out of them in three pieces while this line read as
# withheld. `app.config.Settings` therefore treats this as
# optional and reads a blank value as absent — a reveal attempted in a process
# without it fails naming this variable (ADR 0042, as amended).
#
# So this line is not optional for a deployment. It is optional per *process*,
# and the two that do without it are the two Compose withholds it from.
CARE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://${DB_CARE_USER}:${DB_CARE_PASSWORD}@db:5432/${DB_NAME}

# Celery broker and result backend. The Compose service name, for the same
# reason as above.
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0

# The key both entry doors sign the session JWT with (E1-08, ADR 0089). It is a
# secret: the placeholder below is not a real key, and a deployment generates its
# own with `secrets.token_urlsafe(32)`, kept in `.env` or the secret store and
# nowhere else (SPEC §10). `app.config.Settings` holds it as a `SecretStr`, so it
# is masked in every rendering. It is symmetric — the tool both issues and
# verifies the session — and must be the *same* across the api container and any
# replica, so a restart does not log a sitting person out; a per-process value
# would (which is why the retired login cookie's secret was one, ADR 0078).
SESSION_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-token-urlsafe-32-secret

# --- the AI providers, and there are two of them (E2-12, ADR 0118) -----------
#
# Six variables, in two triples. `AI_PROVIDER_*` describes the real provider —
# any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (§7.4): a hosted provider, a proxy, or a local
# server such as vLLM or Ollama. `MOCK_AI_PROVIDER_*` describes the mock
# `docker-compose.yml` starts (E2-07), which is not a model.
#
# **Why two rather than three variables that switch.** Three cannot describe two
# endpoints. Until E2-12 the only way to reach a real provider was to overwrite
# the values the development stack needs, and the only way to keep the
# development stack working was to have no real provider configured at all —
# while SPEC §9.3's eval floors have to be measured against the real one, on a
# developer's machine as much as in CI. Both are configured at once now, and
# `AIGateway(live=...)` chooses: `live=True` reads the real triple in every
# environment, `live=False` reads the mock's in development and the real one in a
# deployment.
#
# **The real triple may never name `mock-ai`, in any environment.** A base URL
# whose host is that Compose service is refused at startup, exactly as one naming
# `mock-idp` is (ADR 0077, ADR 0113, ADR 0118). The mock runs in every deployment
# that runs the base Compose file, it answers a verdict to any completion, and it
# answers whatever the comment it is sent asks it for — so a stack pointed at it
# stores a character count as a classification under a real prompt version and a
# real model id, and a *local eval run* pointed at it records that count as SPEC
# §9.3's precision and recall floor.
#
# **Off this machine means https, with or without a credential — outside
# development.** Plain http is accepted to an endpoint on this machine
# (`localhost` or a loopback address) in every environment, where nothing crosses
# a network; anywhere else a deployment refuses it rather than put the comment
# being classified, and any credential sent with it, on the wire in the clear
# (§10). A model reached over plain http inside a private network or a cluster is
# not an exception to that: terminate TLS at the model, or run the model
# alongside this application. Development is exempt because a developer with a
# model server on the next machine is a real situation — the same exemption the
# four OIDC URLs below carry, and ADR 0113 records it.
#
# **A deployment sets the two lines below and the key.** The values here name a
# real vendor's endpoint and one of its models, so that the file documents the
# shape of a working configuration rather than a placeholder somebody has to
# decode; nothing in an ordinary run of this stack reaches them, because
# everything an ordinary run does is `live=False` in development.
#
# **The endpoint has to serve the output mode the gateway speaks**, which is not
# a free choice among OpenAI-compatible providers. `app.ai.gateway` asks for
# `response_format: {"type": "json_schema", …, "strict": true}` and declares no
# tool, because SPEC §7.4 requires "one call in, one validated object out — no
# tool use" (ADR 0053). A provider that answers HTTP 400 to that shape cannot be
# used here whatever else it supports, and one was measured doing exactly that
# on 2026-09-02 (ADR 0118's consequences). Check structured outputs before
# naming a provider on these two lines.
#
# **The model is a dated snapshot rather than the floating alias, and that is on
# purpose.** SPEC §9.3's floors are a claim about a particular model: ADR 0031
# records the provider's own identifier on every classification so that two runs
# can be told apart, and the same property has to hold for the model a floor was
# measured on. `tests/evals/validity/floors.py` was measured against
# `gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07` — what the alias `gpt-5-mini` resolved to on 2026-09-02
# — so naming the alias here would let the model move underneath a fixed floor,
# and a breach would then be indistinguishable from a provider swapping the
# weights. Moving this pin is re-measuring the floors, in a pull request whose
# subject is moving them.
AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
AI_PROVIDER_MODEL_NAME=gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07

# The real provider's credential, read by `app.ai.gateway` and by nothing else.
# It is a secret: a real key belongs in `.env` or in the deployment's secret
# store and nowhere else (SPEC §10, CLAUDE.md). `app.config.Settings` holds it as
# a `SecretStr`, so it is masked in every rendering that object has, and the
# gateway raises no error with the provider's own exception chained behind it — a
# chained cause's message reaches the container log too.
#
# **It is blank here on purpose, and blank is a value rather than a gap.** A
# blank key is read as absent, and the request then carries an inert placeholder
# bearer token rather than an empty one — which is what an endpoint that
# authenticates nobody ignores and what some of them refuse. An ordinary run of
# this stack never reaches this provider, so a clean checkout needs no key; a
# placeholder here would read as a value somebody has to replace, and a real one
# would be a credential in the repository. Fill it in your own `.env` when you
# run the eval floors locally (`make evals`), which is the one command that
# reaches this endpoint from a developer's machine.
#
# **The transport rule on AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL above does not read this
# variable.** Off this machine means https whether or not a credential is set,
# because the comment being classified is in the body of every request either
# way. Setting a key adds a second secret to that connection; it does not create
# the requirement.
AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY=

# The mock provider's triple. **These three are what an ordinary run of this
# stack uses**, so `docker compose up` from a clean checkout classifies a comment
# without calling a model anybody pays for, and CI's e2e job — which copies this
# file to `.env` verbatim — runs against it. `mock-ai/README.md` states what the
# service decides: markers a comment can carry to drive a 503, a 500, a malformed
# answer or a stall, and otherwise SPEC §3.3's own character rule.
#
# Read in development and test and nowhere else, so no rule attaches to them:
# this base URL is *meant* to name `mock-ai`, and a deployment that copies this
# file forward without editing these three lines is not misconfigured, because
# nothing there consults them. `app.config.Settings` carries the same values as
# defaults for the same reason.
MOCK_AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=http://mock-ai:8000/v1
MOCK_AI_PROVIDER_MODEL_NAME=mock-validity-v1

# The mock endpoint's credential, blank for the reason the real one is: the mock
# authenticates nobody, so a blank value read as absent is the correct
# configuration rather than a missing one. It exists so that a developer who
# points MOCK_AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL at something else — a colleague's model
# server, a proxy — has somewhere to put its key, and it is masked exactly as the
# real one is, because which of the two is real is not something this repository
# can know.
MOCK_AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY=

# IANA timezone the survey window follows (§3.1). Rejected at startup if the
# runtime cannot resolve it.
INSTITUTION_TIMEZONE=America/New_York

# Free-form deployment name, reported by /healthz. The spec does not constrain
# the vocabulary; `development`, `staging`, and `production` are conventions.
#
# **The identity provider entries below are read against this one.** Anything
# other than `development` is a deployment, and in a deployment a value naming
# the in-repo mock provider is refused at startup (ADR 0077).
ENVIRONMENT=development

# The identity provider the web login door verifies against (§2's second door).
# Five entries, and none of them is a credential: the client is public, it holds
# no secret, and PKCE is what binds an authorization code to it (RFC 7636). So
# there is no OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET here, and its absence is the design rather than
# an omission.
#
# **Required, and refused when they name the mock.** These were defaulted until
# ADR 0077, which reverses that half of ADR 0075: `docker-compose.yml` starts
# `mock-idp` in every deployment, so a deployment that set none of them was
# pointed at a provider that signs an `id_token` for any identity it is asked for
# — CARE and ADMIN included — and verified it correctly. Now a process given no
# provider stops at startup naming the variable, and outside
# `ENVIRONMENT=development` a URL whose host is `mock-idp`, or a client id equal
# to `mock-idp-client`, is refused as well.
#
# **Three more things a deployment cannot set**, all refused at startup and all
# irrelevant to the development values below, so this file needs no edit to
# satisfy them. Outside `ENVIRONMENT=development`:
#
#   * OIDC_AUTHORIZATION_ENDPOINT may not name this machine — `localhost` or any
#     loopback address. That value is not resolved by the container; it is handed
#     to a browser and resolved on the end user's computer, so loopback there is
#     whatever that person happens to be running, on a link your institution
#     issued. It is the one of the five where "this machine" means someone else's.
#   * none of the four URLs may be plain `http` to another host. Anyone on the
#     path can answer the key-set fetch with a key set of their own and mint
#     identities this application then verifies correctly. Plain `http` to a
#     provider on this machine is still fine — there is no wire to read — which is
#     the same exemption AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL above has.
#   * a trailing dot does not evade any of this: `mock-idp.` is `mock-idp`.
#
# The escape hatch for all of them is `ENVIRONMENT=development`, and there is no
# configuration flag that switches one off on its own.
#
# The values below are this repository's own development stack, and they are also
# what `docker-compose.yml` falls back to when a variable is unset, so
# `docker compose up` from a clean checkout still reaches a launchable,
# loggable-into system with no `.env` edits at all (SPEC §14.3). What is set here
# wins over that fallback.
#
# **Two horizons, decided per value rather than per service.** A browser on the
# host reaches these services on published ports at `localhost`; the `api`
# container reaches them by Compose service name. A value a browser is
# *redirected to* is `localhost`; a value the tool *fetches* is the service name.
# Getting this backwards produces a stack that passes every in-process test and
# sends a real browser to a name it cannot resolve. The issuer is neither: it is
# the `iss` an `id_token` must state, compared as a string (OIDC Core 1.0
# §3.1.3.7), so it stays the name the provider calls itself by.
OIDC_ISSUER=http://mock-idp:8000
OIDC_AUTHORIZATION_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:8081/oidc/authorize
OIDC_TOKEN_ENDPOINT=http://mock-idp:8000/oidc/token
OIDC_JWKS_URL=http://mock-idp:8000/.well-known/jwks.json
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=mock-idp-client

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaulted — optional, and these values apply when unset. Why each one has a
# default differs, so the reason is on the entry rather than in this heading.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The tool's own address (E0-18). It is an address a browser is sent to, and its
# default is this repository's own development stack — the value
# `docker-compose.yml` and `docker-compose.override.yml` actually run with, so
# `docker compose up` from a clean checkout reaches a launchable system with no
# `.env` edits at all (SPEC §14.3). It cannot resolve in a deployment, and it
# names no service the base Compose file starts, so a deployment that forgets it
# gets a launch that fails at its first hop rather than a system that is quietly
# wrong. See docs/adr/0075.
#
# The web door's five `OIDC_*` entries used to be in this block. They are
# required now and sit in the section above, because the argument in the
# paragraph above did not hold for them: `mock-idp` is a service every deployment
# starts, so their default did resolve. See docs/adr/0077.
#
# The LTI platform's browser-facing authorization endpoint used to be here too,
# and it is no longer a setting at all. It is a column on the platform's
# registration as of E1-05: one process-wide address is wrong the moment two
# platforms are registered, because a launch from the second would be sent to the
# first's endpoint. `make seed` writes the development value into the row.

# Browser-facing base URL of this tool. `/lti/launch` and `/auth/oidc/callback`
# are derived from it, and both mocks compare the result exactly against the
# address they were registered with — so this and the two `MOCK_*` overrides in
# docker-compose.override.yml move together.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Root log level: CRITICAL, ERROR, WARNING, INFO, or DEBUG. This project's
# choice, not the spec's — §6.3 enumerates the configuration surface and a log
# level is not in it.
#
# Read twice, and deliberately so: by `app.config.Settings` for the application,
# and by `docker-compose.yml`, which passes it to `celery worker` and
# `celery beat` as `--loglevel`. Turning the stack up to DEBUG is one edit.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Responses in a reporting week below which raw comments stay hidden from
# instructors and students alike (§4, §4.1 invariant 3). Configurable, default 5.
N_THRESHOLD_DEFAULT=5

# Benchmark minimum-N (§5.1), distinct from the section small-N above. SPEC §11
# open question 1 leaves the numbers unsettled and suggests these as starting
# values; expect them to move once there is real data behind them.
BENCHMARK_MIN_SECTIONS_DEFAULT=3
BENCHMARK_MIN_RESPONDENTS_DEFAULT=15
